  <h1>"Trulicity Gave Me My A1C Back — and My Confidence"</h1>
  <div class="article-meta"><span>Feb 8, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>8 min read</span></div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>At a glance:</strong> David T., 58, was diagnosed with type 2 diabetes in his late 40s and spent nearly a decade struggling with oral medications that weren't doing enough. After starting Trulicity (dulaglutide), his A1C dropped from 9.1 to 6.4 over 18 months. He lost 22 lbs without trying to, and for the first time in years, stopped dreading his blood work appointments.
  </div>

  <h2>A decade of feeling like I was failing</h2>

  <p>I was diagnosed with type 2 diabetes at 48. Looking back, I probably had it for years before that — I just avoided going to the doctor. When I finally did, my A1C was 8.6 and my GP put me on metformin straight away.</p>

  <p>Metformin did something. It brought my numbers down a bit — A1C hovered around 7.8 for a couple of years. But it never got me where I needed to be, and the digestive side effects were miserable. I was on the maximum dose and still not hitting my target.</p>

  <p>Over the next few years, my doctor added a sulfonylurea, then swapped it for a different one when the first stopped working. My A1C crept back up. By the time I was 56, I was on two oral medications, my A1C was 9.1, and my doctor was having the insulin conversation.</p>

  <blockquote><p>Every blood test felt like getting a school report. And I was always failing. No matter what I did, the numbers kept getting worse.</p></blockquote>

  <p>The worst part wasn't the numbers themselves. It was the shame. I felt like I was doing something wrong — eating too much, not exercising enough, not trying hard enough. Every three-month blood test felt like a verdict on my character. My wife could tell when results day was coming because I'd go quiet for days beforehand.</p>

  <h2>Why Trulicity, not Ozempic</h2>

  <p>My doctor suggested a GLP-1 medication before moving to insulin. I'd heard of Ozempic from the news — hard not to — but she recommended Trulicity instead. Her reasoning was straightforward: for my situation, which was primarily about blood sugar control rather than weight loss, Trulicity had a strong evidence base and she'd had good results with her other diabetes patients on it.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>About Trulicity:</strong> Trulicity (dulaglutide) is a GLP-1 receptor agonist made by Eli Lilly, approved for type 2 diabetes management. It's a once-weekly injection, like Ozempic, but uses a different active ingredient. While it's not as widely discussed as Ozempic or Mounjaro in the media, it's one of the most prescribed GLP-1 medications for diabetes globally. It's not currently approved specifically for weight loss.
  </div>

  <p>I'll be honest — I was relieved it wasn't insulin. That felt like a line I wasn't ready to cross. The idea of a weekly injection that could improve my blood sugar without the complexity of insulin dosing was appealing. My doctor was clear that this wasn't a guarantee, and that if it didn't work, insulin was still on the table. But it was worth trying.</p>

  <h2>Getting started</h2>

  <p>The Trulicity pen is probably the easiest injection device I've ever seen. You click it against your stomach or thigh, press a button, wait for the click, and you're done. The needle is hidden — you never see it. For someone who'd been dreading injections, it was a non-event.</p>

  <p>I started on 0.75mg. Side effects were mild — some nausea for the first week or two, a bit of an upset stomach. Nothing compared to what metformin had put me through for years. By week three, I felt completely normal.</p>

  <p>The first thing I noticed wasn't my blood sugar. It was that I was less hungry between meals. I wasn't ravenous by mid-afternoon anymore. I stopped reaching for biscuits with my tea out of habit. I didn't set out to lose weight, but I was naturally eating less without thinking about it.</p>

  <h2>The numbers that changed everything</h2>

  <p>My first A1C test after starting Trulicity was at three months. I'd been dreading it, as usual. My wife drove me to the surgery and waited in the car.</p>

  <p>7.4.</p>

  <p>Down from 9.1. In three months. On a single medication change.</p>

  <p>I sat in the doctor's office and didn't know what to say. She smiled and said, "This is what was supposed to happen. Your diabetes wasn't a willpower problem — you just needed the right tool."</p>

  <p>That sentence changed something in me. For a decade I'd been blaming myself. And here was this medication doing what all my guilt and effort couldn't.</p>

