  <h2>The side effects — honest version</h2>

  <p>I've seen horror stories online about GLP-1 side effects, so I want to be upfront about my experience. Trulicity was gentle for me. That doesn't mean it will be for everyone, but here's exactly what I dealt with:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weeks 1–3:</strong> Mild nausea, mostly in the mornings. Manageable — I ate a small breakfast and it passed. Nothing close to what I'd experienced on metformin.</li>
    <li><strong>Week 4 onward (0.75mg):</strong> Side effects essentially gone. Felt completely normal.</li>
    <li><strong>Dose increase to 1.5mg (month 3):</strong> Slight return of nausea for about a week, then settled again. Some constipation that I managed by drinking more water and eating more fibre.</li>
    <li><strong>Months 6–18:</strong> No ongoing side effects. Injection site occasionally itches for an hour. That's it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The one thing I will flag: I had a couple of episodes of mild hypoglycaemia in the first few months, which my doctor said was likely because of the combination with the sulfonylurea. She discontinued the sulfonylurea after my six-month results came back, and I haven't had an episode since.</p>

  <h2>What it's actually like living on Trulicity</h2>

  <p>The day-to-day reality is remarkably unremarkable — and I mean that as the highest compliment. Every Thursday evening, I do my injection. It takes about 10 seconds. I put the pen back in the fridge. And that's it until next Thursday.</p>

  <p>My appetite is lower but not gone. I still enjoy food — I still look forward to meals, I still eat out with my wife, I still have a Sunday roast. I just eat less. The portions that used to feel normal now feel like more than enough. I leave food on my plate sometimes, which is something I never did before.</p>

  <blockquote><p>For the first time in ten years, I don't dread my blood work. I actually look forward to it — because I know the numbers will be good. That's a feeling I never thought I'd have.</p></blockquote>

  <p>The weight loss has been gradual and steady — 22 lbs over 18 months. I wasn't trying to lose weight; it just happened as a consequence of eating less. My doctor said that's typical with Trulicity — the weight loss is more modest than what you see with Ozempic or Mounjaro, but it's real and it helps with the diabetes management.</p>

  <h2>The conversation about Ozempic</h2>

  <p>About a year in, I asked my doctor whether I should switch to Ozempic or Mounjaro. By that point they were everywhere in the news, and I wondered if I was on the "wrong" medication.</p>

  <p>She was reassuring. She said Trulicity was doing exactly what it needed to do for me. My A1C was 6.5, my blood sugar was stable, my side effects were minimal. Switching to chase a slightly bigger number on the weight loss side didn't make sense when everything was working. If my A1C started climbing again or if I needed more weight loss support in the future, we'd revisit it.</p>

  <p>That made sense to me. There's a lot of noise about GLP-1 medications right now, and it's easy to feel like you're missing out if you're not on the latest, most talked-about one. But the best medication is the one that works for you, not the one with the biggest headlines.</p>

  <h2>What I wish I'd known earlier</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Diabetes isn't a character flaw.</strong> I spent a decade blaming myself for numbers that were largely driven by biology. GLP-1 medications work on your hormones, not your willpower. If oral medications aren't enough, that's not a failure — it's just biology.</li>
    <li><strong>Trulicity deserves more attention.</strong> It's not as famous as Ozempic, but for straightforward type 2 diabetes management, it's a serious medication with a strong track record. Don't dismiss it because it's not trending on social media.</li>
    <li><strong>The injection is nothing.</strong> I was anxious about it for weeks before starting. The pen is so simple and the needle so small that my wife didn't believe I'd actually done it the first time. If fear of injections is holding you back, I promise — it's nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask about your medication combination.</strong> The hypoglycaemia episodes I had were caused by the interaction between Trulicity and my sulfonylurea, not Trulicity alone. Once my doctor adjusted the other medications, the problem went away entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Give it time.</strong> My A1C didn't reach its best level until month 12. The improvement was gradual and steady. If you're not seeing dramatic results at month two, that doesn't mean it isn't working.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Trulicity vs. the newer GLP-1s:</strong> Trulicity, Ozempic, and Mounjaro are all GLP-1 receptor agonists, but they have different active ingredients and different approved uses. Trulicity (dulaglutide) is approved specifically for type 2 diabetes. Ozempic (semaglutide) is approved for diabetes with weight loss as a common benefit. Mounjaro (tirzepatide) is a dual-agonist approved for diabetes. Your doctor can help determine which is the best fit based on your specific situation and goals.
  </div>

  <h2>Where I am now</h2>

  <p>Eighteen months in and I feel like a different person. Not physically — though 22 lbs lighter and an A1C of 6.4 is nothing to dismiss. The real change is mental. I don't carry the weight of failure around anymore. I go to my blood tests without dread. I talk about my diabetes openly instead of hiding it.</p>

  <p>My doctor recently reduced my metformin dose because my numbers are so well controlled. That's a sentence I never imagined writing. For the first time in a decade, I'm moving in the right direction — not because I found some hidden reserve of willpower, but because I finally got the right tool for the job.</p>

  <p>If you're sitting where I was — on multiple oral medications, watching your A1C climb, feeling like you're losing a battle — please talk to your doctor about GLP-1 options. It doesn't have to be Trulicity. It doesn't have to be Ozempic. But it might be the thing that changes everything.</p>

